 are still running our  economy on oil because the people who  make those things and sell those things  make enormous profits off them but there  is change underway change that will  transform the urban landscape we are  about to see a wave of battles around  smart growth in metropolitan politics  about energy use about fuel standards  mileage standards and other  performance-based attributes of cars and  buildings and other things that are  gonna really fundamentally put people  who are advocating for bright green  cities as the future in direct  opposition to people who are advocating  for more of the status quo of sprawl and  high energy use Stefan believes he has  the blueprint for change how do you  start thinking about how to bring  solutions to bear to really radically  change the nature of the future part of  the idea behind really starting to talk  about bright green solutions was to  emphasize that the future can be both  green and bright right that it's we  don't need you know to think about this  in terms of decline and in terms of you  know the erosion of things we love or  our quality of life or sacrifice that  there are better ways to think about it  so we invent a new model of what  prosperity can look like then there's  another game in town and those four  billion people who are coming up rising  out of poverty can choose to emulate  that getting to bright green requires  systemic changes in the way we think  about the future the reason why I say  that the biggest barrier to building a  bright green future maybe in our heads  is that most of the time when we think  about the way things can be we in fact  think about the way things are if you  think in terms of the system as it  exists now being a given it's really  hard to imagine the kind of shifts that  we need to see happening and you get  into a situation where you start to  do what we call the swamp right where  you start to go oh well the system as a  whole can't change right everybody's  still gonna drive everywhere so let's  see if we can't invent you know a carbon  neutral car right thinking that that  will solve the problem  of course unfortunately it doesn't solve  the problem it's not a big enough shift  to actually get us where we need to go  thinking in terms of that established  system also has the difficulty of  locking us into decisions that we maybe  don't want to actually be making if we  had a different system we could in fact  change that game right we know that it's  possible for example to rebuild our  cities so there are more compact  communities with more choices in terms  of how people get around more  walkability  more access to things one needs nearby  it means that people drive less people  tend to use less energy in their homes  they tend to be able to live in smaller  spaces much more comfortably they can  really spend less money on stuff because  if you have a you know 2,000 square foot  home instead of a 5,000 square-foot home  you don't need to buy as much crap right  and so in general compact communities  facilitate all sorts of better decisions  Stephon believes technology and  information can help us change the way  we think about and use energy we know  that bringing intelligence to  decision-making into systems and  transparency for users into what's  happening with those systems changes the  way people act right that when we are  able to measure things for instance we  use them differently when you bring an  energy meter inside the house and you  can see their energy use on a daily  basis their energy use drops somewhere  between 7 and 12 percent without  anything else happening in the house so  for example smart grids which allow  energy users to essentially network with  one another and their utility much the  same way that people network with  information on the Internet  right so you can have a smart meter that  both tracks how much energy you're using  coming in and helps you use that energy  more effectively but also helps you feed  energy back into the system if you have  what's called net metering  so it can if you have a solar panel on  your roof when you're generating more  energy with your solar panel than you  need it feeds it back in the system and  your energy meter run backwards right  that's a pretty cool piece of technology  you can add that as well to electric  cars they in effect are big batteries on  wheels and when you have a whole bunch  of batteries hooked up to you know a  smart grid you end up in a position  where you have enormous amounts of  stored energy that when you have these  energy surges and peaks and demand can  be fed back into the system right and  then can be recharged when there's less  need for the energy when energy is  cheaper which basically means you don't  need to build as many power plants you  don't need to have as much centralized  energy running that system yet  technology alone is not the answer to a  sustainable future there is I think a  real tendency which is unfortunate to  assume that because we are making  technological progress in terms of  better understanding various capacities  of technologies and materials and so  forth that we are necessarily making  progress towards sustainability that's  not always the case it's also true that  some of these things potentially have  some very severe ecological and social  costs of their own that we haven't yet  recipe right there is some preliminary  data that suggests that certain  nanomaterials may not be safe for  instance that said there are also a  series of fields in which we're seeing  innovation that would appear to maybe  have more potential for good vennett's  yet delivering I think that one of the  real places that that's happening is in  terms of ubiquitous computing right the  idea that more and more things will have  computer technology embedded in them  right  have the ability to measure things and  communicate what they're measuring or  what they're seeing what they're feeling  right and the sort of social  technologies that that we've seen emerge  which are really still - in their  infancy and we've a vaccine already some  great examples of people coming together  in networks and working collaboratively  to deliver you know medical  breakthroughs to design new products to  come up with new software that works  better and it's really available I mean  there's there's more and more examples  of people working together in an  open-source manner to solve problems  world changing 2.0 offers a template for  enacting a bright green future for  everyone  fundamentally we know what makes us  happiest - it's good relationships with  people meaningful work you know sense of  belonging a sense of security and safety  interesting  and right now much of much of our lives  mouthing requiring stuff that gets in  the way  I think we're gonna start to see instead  on a shift towards those things  themselves has the goals right I also  think that we're gonna start to see  something else which is that  fundamentally you can't lead a good life  an ethically good life a life that feels  good and know that you are depriving  future generations of the opportunity to  live that way as well  for more information on the visions of a  sustainable world program at the Yale  School of Forestry and environmental  studies please visit environment  yale.edu slash visions
 

